From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Christopher Owen <3221756+christopherowen@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 27 Nov 2026 23:55:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] RoCE: order eager collectives after graph replays

Upstream base: christopherowen/dgx-spark-networking@b01f350d061a6434c2186df73fa568ab4a9e4aa0
(0.6.0, no local series before this).

Rationale: the one-shot transport has one epoch, one doorbell and two
ping-pong slots, so collectives must execute in launch order even across
streams. `_order_stream`/`_mark_stream` enforce that for eager<->eager, but
`_mark_stream(capturing=True)` records nothing, so a graph replay is
invisible: under async scheduling (vLLM `--async-scheduling`), alt-stream
eager SP prefill collectives overlap main-stream decode replays on the same
epoch/slot region. Measured on spark-eb1a (experiments/2026-11-27-sp-
collective-blocks): at `SPARKNET_ROCE_COLLECTIVE_BLOCKS=16` the race shows
as one logprob ULP per generation; at 64 (full width) one token of 192
flips; hashes drift run-to-run; single-stream generations are immune; the
default 8-CTA cap masks the window. The fix: when an eager collective runs
on a different stream than the last capture, record an event on the capture
stream (the record enqueues after every replay submitted to it so far) and
make the eager stream wait it, restoring global launch order at the
captured<->eager boundary.

Quality: pure stream-ordering; no kernel, buffer, protocol or arithmetic
change. The extra wait is one event per capture->eager transition, off the
collective's critical path (the eager launch already waits the last eager
collective).

Tests: the 2026-11-27-sp-collective-blocks probe receipts (runs b1 at widths
0/16) reproduce the failure pre-patch; qualification is two clean boots at
full width with determinism hashes equal to the promoted stack's and prefill
at the proven 8,625 tok/s level.

Upstream status: local series first; contribution-back candidate (the 8-CTA
cap currently masks this race for every async-scheduling deployment).
---
 sparknet/oneshot/runtime.py | 14 ++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 12 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/sparknet/oneshot/runtime.py b/sparknet/oneshot/runtime.py
--- a/sparknet/oneshot/runtime.py
+++ b/sparknet/oneshot/runtime.py
@@ -467,6 +467,8 @@
         self._gather_buffers: Optional[tuple[torch.Tensor, torch.Tensor]] = None
         self._align_buffers: Optional[tuple[torch.Tensor, torch.Tensor]] = None
         self._stream_event = torch.cuda.Event()
+        self._replay_event = torch.cuda.Event()
+        self._replay_pending = False
         self._last_stream: Optional[torch.cuda.Stream] = None
         self._capture_stream: Optional[torch.cuda.Stream] = None
         self._capture_id = 0
@@ -1121,6 +1123,15 @@
                     "RoCE collectives must all be captured on one stream"
                 )
             return
+        if self._capture_stream is not None and current != self._capture_stream:
+            # A captured launch used the transport after (or before) the last
+            # eager one; a graph replay is invisible to the eager ordering
+            # above, so record on the capture stream -- the record enqueues
+            # after every replay submitted to it so far -- and wait it here.
+            if self._replay_pending:
+                self._replay_event.record(self._capture_stream)
+                self._replay_pending = False
+            current.wait_event(self._replay_event)
         if self._last_stream is not None and current != self._last_stream:
             current.wait_event(self._stream_event)
 
@@ -1128,6 +1139,7 @@
         """Record the ordering event on the current stream after a launch."""
 
         if capturing:
+            self._replay_pending = True
             return
         current = torch.cuda.current_stream(self.device)
         self._stream_event.record(current)
